regulation.\",5\r\n4000,DESMOPRESINA,\"Desmopresina is a synthetic medication that serves multiple purposes in the medical field. Primarily, it is used in the treatment of diabetes insipidus, especially in its central form, where there is a deficiency of the antidiuretic hormone (ADH). Additionally, Desmopresina plays a role in managing sodium levels, particularly in conjunction with tolvaptán, when sodium levels rise too quickly. It is also utilized in the diagnosis and treatment of conditions related to ADH deficiency and is effective in managing hyponatremia. Overall, Desmopresina is a versatile medication that addresses both the symptoms of diabetes insipidus and the complications associated with sodium imbalances.\",4\r\n3753,DIABETES INSÍPIDA,\"Diabetes insípida is a medical condition characterized by excessive thirst (polydipsia) and the excretion of large amounts of severely diluted urine (polyuria), leading to an imbalance in body fluids. This disorder arises from the body's inability to concentrate urine, often due to dysfunctions in the hypothalamus or pituitary gland. It can be associated with neurogenic causes, such as necrosis of the neurohypophysis, and may also occur as a result of untreated adrenal insufficiency. In some cases, it is linked to the presence of pituitary adenomas or hypothalamic damage due to pineal tumors, which disrupt the regulation of water in the body. The condition is discussed in the context of treatment options, including glucocorticoid replacement therapy, as outlined in the Manual de Endocrinología y Nutrición.\",17\r\n3688,OXITOCINA,\"OXITOCINA is a hormone produced in the hypothalamus and released by the neurohypophysis. It plays a crucial role in childbirth and lactation, facilitating these essential physiological processes. Additionally, oxytocin exhibits antidiuretic effects, contributing to various other physiological functions within the body.\",2\r\n3677,NEUROHIPÓFISIS,\"The neurohipófisis, or neurohypophysis, is the